<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liu Yin (Southern Han)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">874–911</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liu Yin (874–911) was a Chinese general of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> who became military governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingnan">Lingnan</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> in the early 10th century. From a family enriched by the southern trade, he built up an autonomous regional power that the Later Liang dynasty recognised by making him Prince of Nanhai. After his death his brother Liu Yan turned it into the Southern Han kingdom.</p></div>
